**This session's net progress** (on the `2 node dlm=tcp` durable-directory-loss blocker):

- **Criterion not met; marker correctly unwritten.** The green 16/16 suite is a flaky pass over a reproducible directory-entry loss (I reproduced shorts on the deployed build multiple times).
- **Refuted four distinct fix approaches with evidence** (so they aren't re-tried): merge-via-extra-lock (crashes FS), merge-with-I/O-under-lock (wedges handoff, perf-rule violation), read-side/FUA (no effect), and force-block-format (no effect — the shortform→block "transition" was a red herring; the pre-grown test was clean only due to multi-block spread).
- **Got a GPT-5.5 architectural consult**: treat the DLM grant as buffer-cache ownership (checkpoint-on-handoff + epoch-checked writeback); for hot dirs, owner-delegation/op-forwarding — *not* stale-buffer merging.
- **Reconciled with prior instrumented evidence**: the loss is at insert/conversion time (first entry absent on both nodes' earliest block image), not stale post-handoff writeback.
- **Surfaced a critical new question at the very end**: a short that reported 199/200 (with both recovery probes failing *at that instant*) **reappeared as 200/200 minutes later** — suggesting the bug may be a **transient visibility/durability lag, not permanent data loss**. My earlier "durable, unrecoverable" verdicts never re-checked after a delay. The baseline transience test I ran didn't trigger a short in 20 iters (it uses fresh dirs, less aggressive than the daddr-reuse probe), so **this remains unconfirmed** and is the #1 thing to resolve next.

